{number: 58}
{title: I Love to Tell the Story}
{key: C}

{start_of_verse: 1}
[C] I love to tell the story of [F] unseen things a-[C] bove,
Of Jesus and His glory, of [G7] Jesus and His [C] love.
I love to tell the story, be-[F] cause I know 'tis [C] true;
It satisfies my longings as [G7] nothing else could [C] do.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_chorus}
I [G7] love to tell the [C] story,
'Twill [F] be my theme in [C] glory,
To [F] tell the old, old [C] story
Of [G7] Jesus and His [C] love.
{end_of_chorus}

{start_of_verse: 2}
[C] I love to tell the story; more [F] wonderful it [C] seems
Than all the golden fancies of [G7] all our golden [C] dreams.
I love to tell the story, it [F] did so much for [C] me;
And that is just the reason I [G7] tell it now to [C] thee.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: 3}
[C] I love to tell the story; 'tis [F] pleasant to re-[C] peat
What seems, each time I tell it, more [G7] wonderfully [C] sweet.
I love to tell the story, for [F] some have never [C] heard
The message of salvation from [G7] God's own holy [C] Word.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: 4}
[C] I love to tell the story, for [F] those who know it [C] best
Seem hungering and thirsting to [G7] hear it like the [C] rest.
And when, in scenes of glory, I [F] sing the new, new [C] song,
'Twill be the old, old story that [G7] I have loved so [C] long.
{end_of_verse}
